<div class="book-title">Likutay Halachos — Orach Chaim</div>

<!-- ==================== SEUDAH HALACHAH 1 ==================== -->

<div class="halacha-title">Hilchos Seudah — Halachah 1<br><span style="font-size:1rem;font-weight:400;">Wine Exempts All Beverages</span></div>
<div class="lm-subtitle">Based on <span class="v">"Ha'i man d'azil l'minsav"</span> <span class="sr">(<span class="lr">LM I, 29</span>)</span></div>

<div class="sn">Wine, Blood, and the Rectification of <i>Da'as</i></div>

<div class="p">See the teaching <span class="v">"One who goes to marry…"</span> (<span class="lr">LM I, 29</span>), where it is explained that the rectification of all the <span class="h">damim</span> [bloods] is achieved by elevating the <span class="h">moach</span> [brain/mind]. From there, a whiteness flows into all the bloods.</div>

<div class="p">The <span class="h">berachah</span> on every item serves to strengthen and rectify one's <span class="h">nefesh</span>. Through this, one can rectify the <span class="h">nefesh</span> clothed within the food. This is why our Sages forbade eating before prayer, deriving it from: <span class="v">"You shall not eat upon the blood"</span> — <span class="mx">"do not eat before you pray for your blood."</span> <span class="v">"For the blood is the <span class="h">nefesh</span>"</span> — one must pray first to rectify his blood and <span class="h">nefesh</span>. Then he can eat and elevate the <span class="h">nefashos</span> clothed in the food. Prayer consists of songs, praises, blessings, and thanksgivings — through these the <span class="h">nefashos</span> are rectified. For the <span class="h">nefesh</span> is the aspect of <span class="h">dibbur</span> [speech], and all <span class="h">nefashos</span> clothed in everything are aspects of letters and speech. The <span class="h">tikkun</span> of speech is through praising the <span class="h">tzadikim</span> and blessing Hashem — the <span class="h">Tzadik</span> of the world.</div>

<div class="p">This is also the <span class="h">berachah</span> on each item — blessing and thanking the <span class="h">Tzadik</span> of the world, rectifying the <span class="h">nefashos</span> that are aspects of speech. The <span class="h">Tikkunim</span> state: <span class="h">berachah</span> is from the side of <span class="h">Imma Ila'ah</span> — <span class="v">"to bring blessing to your house"</span> — for there the blood is purified and becomes milk = the rectification of the <span class="h">nefashos</span>.</div>

<div class="p">When one blesses over wine, the <span class="h">da'as</span> is greatly elevated: <span class="mx">"if one merits — it becomes a head"</span> [= expanded mind]. Through the <span class="h">berachah</span> = "meriting" = purifying the blood. Therefore <span class="kc">one need not bless again on other beverages</span>: since the <span class="h">da'as</span> has been rectified and elevated through the wine and its blessing, the <span class="h">nefashos</span> (= <span class="h">damim</span>) are automatically rectified — for the rectification of the <span class="h">damim</span> depends on the <span class="h">da'as</span>.</div>

<div class="p">Wine is so significant that one must bless on it even during a meal — bread does not exempt it. Bread too derives its importance from <span class="h">da'as</span> — hence it exempts the entire meal. But wine, whose <span class="h">da'as</span> is even greater, is not exempted by bread.</div>

<div class="p">We bless <span class="h">HaTov V'HaMeitiv</span> on a change of wine — for all good depends on <span class="h">da'as</span>: <span class="v">"also without <span class="h">da'as</span>, the <span class="h">nefesh</span> is not good."</span> Wine = <span class="h">da'as</span>; where there is <span class="h">da'as</span>, there is good.</div>

<div class="sep">✦ ✦ ✦</div>

<!-- ==================== SEUDAH HALACHAH 2 (= BETZI'AS HAPAS) ==================== -->

<div class="halacha-title">Hilchos Seudah — Halachah 2<br><span style="font-size:1rem;font-weight:400;">Bread Establishes Its Own Blessing</span></div>
<div class="lm-subtitle">Based on <span class="v">"Chanukah V'Hoda'ah"</span> <span class="sr">(<span class="lr">LM II, 2</span>)</span></div>

<div class="sn">Blessings on Food: Including This World in the World to Come</div>

<div class="p">The essence: <span class="h">hoda'ah</span> [thanksgiving] is the aspect of the World to Come — for the World to Come is to thank and praise and <span class="kc">know</span> Him. Through this, the <span class="h">dibbur</span> is perfected = <span class="h">lashon hakodesh</span>. Through this, the holiness of Shabbos is drawn into the six weekdays. Through this, the <span class="kc">simple unity</span> is revealed — that all diverse actions flow from the One.</div>

<div class="p">This is <span class="h">Birchas HaNehenin</span> — the obligation to bless before every enjoyment. One must attach his thought to the World to Come and separate from this world entirely — <span class="mx">"fortunate is one who derives no benefit from this world."</span> For every necessity we take from this world (eating, drinking, etc.), we bless — thereby <span class="kc">including and elevating these worldly things into the World to Come</span>. When we bless Hashem for a food, that food becomes a vehicle of praise and thanksgiving = the World to Come aspect. The six weekdays (= this world, a mixture of good and evil) are sanctified by Shabbos (= the World to Come).</div>

<div class="p">Through blessing over the diverse things of this world — each with its different appearance, taste, and scent — we reveal that all these <span class="kc">diverse actions flow from the simple One</span> who created them. This revelation of unity from diversity is exceedingly precious to Him.</div>

<div class="sn">Specific Blessings Are Greater Than General Ones</div>

<div class="p">Now it is perfectly clear why specific blessings are more valued than general ones. The more specifically one blesses — the more the simple unity is revealed from diverse and particular actions. <span class="h">She'hakol</span> [the general blessing] does not reveal the unity from particular diversity as powerfully as <span class="v"><span class="h">Borei Pri HaGafen</span></span> [a specific blessing on wine]. The more particular the action, the more precious is the revelation of His unity through it.</div>

<div class="p">The importance of each food depends on the <span class="h">da'as</span> clothed within it — <span class="v">"wisdom gives life to everything."</span> Where more <span class="h">da'as</span> is clothed, it is more important and merits a more specific blessing. <span class="h">Da'as</span> = the World to Come (= knowing Him, thanking Him). The more <span class="h">da'as</span>, the more one grasps the simple unity.</div>

<div class="sn">Bread and Wine: The Pinnacle</div>

<div class="p">Bread = <span class="h">da'as</span>: <span class="v">"come, eat of my bread."</span> Therefore it is the most important — establishing its own specific blessing. Only the five species of grain are called <span class="h">lechem</span> = the <span class="h">hei</span> of <span class="h">da'as</span>. Bread accompanies the <span class="h">Korban Todah</span> — and <span class="h">todah</span> [thanksgiving] is the World to Come: <span class="mx">"all sacrifices will be nullified except the <span class="h">todah</span>"</span> — in the future, only thanksgiving remains.</div>

<div class="p">Wine too = <span class="h">da'as</span>: <span class="mx">"wine and fragrance sharpen the mind"</span> <span class="sr">(Sanhedrin 70)</span>; <span class="mx">"if one merits — it becomes a head"</span> = expanded <span class="h">mochin</span>.</div>

<div class="p" style="font-style:italic;color:var(--section-num);text-align:center;margin-top:1rem;">[Seudah Halachah 3 — Hilchos Devarim HaNohagin B'Seudah Halachah 2 — see end of Hilchos Pesach Halachah 2.]</div>

<div class="sep">✦ ✦ ✦</div>

<!-- ==================== BETZI'AS HAPAS HALACHAH 3 ==================== -->

<div class="halacha-title">Hilchos Betzi'as HaPas — Halachah 3<br><span style="font-size:1rem;font-weight:400;">The Mystery of Breaking Bread</span></div>
<div class="lm-subtitle">Based on <span class="v">"BaChatzotzeroth"</span> <span class="sr">(<span class="lr">LM I, 5</span>)</span> — on thunder, purifying the mind, and prayer with power.</div>

<div class="sn">Introduction: <i>Chametz</i> and <i>Matzah</i></div>

<div class="p">In the teaching <span class="v">"BaChatzotzeroth"</span> (<span class="lr">LM I, 5</span>), it is explained that one must empty the mind of foreign thoughts and desires — not allowing the mind to become <span class="h">chametz</span> [leavened] with cravings. One must rebuke these thoughts. This is the aspect of <span class="h">matzah</span> — from the root <span class="h">matzah</span> = strife/rebuke: <span class="v">"rebuke the beast of the reed [<span class="h">cheis kaneh</span>]"</span> <span class="sr">(Psalms 68:31)</span>. The Zohar: <span class="mx">break the <span class="h">kaneh</span> of the <span class="h">cheis</span> and make from it a <span class="h">hei</span></span> — transforming the letters of <span class="h">chametz</span> (ח-מ-ץ) into <span class="h">matzah</span> (מ-צ-ה): the <span class="h">cheis</span> is broken into a <span class="h">hei</span>. Guard your wisdom from contamination by foreign wisdoms and evil thoughts — escaping from death to life, from <span class="h">chametz</span> to <span class="h">matzah</span>, from <span class="h">cheis</span> to <span class="h">hei</span>.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 1 — Why "Breaking" Is the Name for the Blessing</div>

<div class="p">Our Sages called the one who blesses <span class="h">HaMotzi</span> by the name <span class="h">"botze'a"</span> [one who breaks], and designated the entire act of blessing by the name of breaking: <span class="mx">"the host breaks and the guest blesses"</span> <span class="sr">(Berachos 46a)</span>. Why not call him "the one who blesses <span class="h">HaMotzi</span>"? Throughout Scripture, eating bread is called "breaking" and "splitting": <span class="v">"children asked for bread — <span class="h">poreis</span> [one who breaks] there is none for them"</span> <span class="sr">(Lamentations 4:4)</span>; <span class="v">"is it not to break [<span class="h">paros</span>] your bread for the hungry?"</span> <span class="sr">(Isaiah 58:7)</span>. The <span class="h">Shulchan Aruch</span> calls all laws of <span class="h">HaMotzi</span> by the name <span class="h">"Hilchos Betzi'as HaPas"</span> — the breaking is the essence.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 2 — Breaking Bread = Transforming <i>Chametz</i> to <i>Matzah</i></div>

<div class="p">Israel's eating must always be in holiness — in the aspect of <span class="h">matzah</span>. <span class="h">Chametz</span> = the side of death; every meal must be food purified from the sin of Adam and the serpent's venom. Through such food, the <span class="h">da'as</span> is perfected. Adam's sin was eating from the Tree of Knowledge of Good and Evil = <span class="h">chametz</span>. Through it the mind was confused, thoughts mixed — good and evil intermingled. The opposite: the Tree of Life = <span class="h">matzah</span> = holy thought = life.</div>

<div class="p">Through Adam's sin the ground was cursed: <span class="v">"in sorrow you shall eat."</span> All foods were blemished — especially bread, the core of human life. Therefore bread requires many labors before it is fit to eat: plowing, sowing, reaping… and there are <span class="kc">ten <span class="h">mitzvos</span> in bread</span> (plowing restrictions, harvesting laws, <span class="h">challah</span>, etc.). Through these <span class="h">mitzvos</span>, bread is purified from the serpent's venom. The final stage of purification: <span class="kc">the <span class="h">berachah</span></span> — extracting bread from curse to blessing, from the ground's curse to elevation through blessing.</div>

<div class="p">When bread is purified for Israel's eating, it is in the aspect of <span class="h">matzah</span> = Tree of Life = holy <span class="h">da'as</span>. Our Sages: <span class="mx">"Torah was given only to eaters of the <span class="h">man</span>"</span> — and <span class="h">man</span> = <span class="h">matzah</span>: <span class="mx">"the <span class="h">ugah</span> they brought from Egypt — they tasted in it the <span class="h">man</span>."</span></div>

<div class="p">Through <span class="h">Birchas HaMotzi</span>, bread is purified. The essence of <span class="h">matzah</span> is breaking the <span class="h">kaneh</span> of the <span class="h">cheis</span> and making a <span class="h">hei</span>. This is the <span class="kc"><span class="h">hei</span> of <span class="h">HaMotzi</span></span> — which one must pronounce distinctly, as our Sages taught <span class="sr">(Berachos 38a)</span>: the <span class="h">hei</span> = <span class="h">matzah</span> = breaking the <span class="h">cheis</span> into a <span class="h">hei</span>.</div>

<div class="p"><span class="kc">This is <span class="h">Betzi'as HaPas</span></span>: one breaks the bread after the <span class="h">berachah</span> to signify that the blessing extracts the bread from <span class="h">chametz</span> to <span class="h">matzah</span> — breaking the <span class="h">cheis</span>'s reed into a <span class="h">hei</span>. <span class="h">Matzah</span> is itself a broken piece (<span class="h">perusah</span>). On Pesach, the middle <span class="h">matzah</span> is broken before the <span class="h">Hagadah</span>, and the blessing is on the broken piece — because <span class="h">matzah</span>'s essence is being broken: the <span class="h">cheis</span> already shattered into <span class="h">hei</span> through guarding the <span class="h">matzah</span> from leavening.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 3 — <i>Lechem Oni</i>: The Poor Man's Bread</div>

<div class="p"><span class="mx">"<span class="h">Lechem oni</span> — what is the poor man's way? With a broken piece"</span> <span class="sr">(Pesachim 116a)</span>. Poverty derives from <span class="v">"in sorrow you shall eat"</span> = Adam's sin = <span class="h">chametz</span>. The poor man is considered as dead (<span class="v">"on the day you eat from it…"</span>). Whoever gives the poor person bread — raises him from poverty, from <span class="h">chametz</span> to <span class="h">matzah</span>. Therefore <span class="h">tzedakah</span> is called <span class="h">perisah</span> [breaking]: <span class="v">"is it not to break your bread for the hungry?"</span> — elevating from <span class="h">chametz</span> to <span class="h">matzah</span>. <span class="h">Tzedakah</span> = the <span class="h">hei</span>: making <span class="h">tzedek</span> into <span class="h">tzedakah</span> by adding <span class="h">hei</span> <span class="sr">(Tikkunei Zohar 21)</span>.</div>

<div class="p">A mourner (<span class="h">aveil</span>) — touched by the side of death — cannot break bread himself; he cannot overcome <span class="h">chametz</span> at that moment. Therefore his first meal must come from others: they break bread for him, elevating him from <span class="h">chametz</span> to <span class="h">matzah</span>.</div>

<div class="p">Another interpretation of <span class="h">lechem oni</span>: <span class="mx">"bread upon which one answers many things"</span> — through <span class="h">matzah</span> (breaking the <span class="h">cheis</span>), one purifies thought and gains the power to <span class="kc">pray with a great voice</span>, with full strength.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 4 — Eating in Holiness: Thunder and Prayer</div>

<div class="p">Eating in holiness = purifying bread from <span class="h">chametz</span> to <span class="h">matzah</span> = gaining the power to pray with force. Human strength and vitality come through eating. Eating derives from <span class="h">Gevuros</span> = <span class="h">Gevuros Geshamim</span> [rains] = thunder. <span class="h">Da'as</span> depends on eating. When one eats in holiness, the voice emerging with power in prayer strikes the mind and becomes thunder — and the heart hears.</div>

<div class="p"><span class="h">Yirah</span> [awe] — from which the voice issues — also comes through holy eating, as explained in <span class="lr">LM II, 7</span>: the essential <span class="h">yirah</span> approaches a person during eating.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 5 — "From Where Do You Begin <i>HaMotzi</i>?"</div>

<div class="p"><span class="mx">"From where do you begin <span class="h">HaMotzi</span>? From where the baking began"</span> <span class="sr">(Sanhedrin 102b)</span>. Through fire/baking, bread is purified from the serpent's venom — <span class="v">"from the fire they went out, and the fire shall consume them"</span> <span class="sr">(Ezekiel 15:7)</span>. One breaks bread from the spot where fire first took hold — there the purification began, there one breaks and blesses = transforming <span class="h">cheis</span> to <span class="h">hei</span> = the <span class="h">hei</span> of <span class="h">HaMotzi</span>.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 6 — <i>Eretz Yisrael</i>, <i>Bnei Cheis</i>, and the <i>Hei</i> of Avraham</div>

<div class="p">The conquest of <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span> began when Avraham purchased the Cave of Machpelah from <span class="h">Bnei Cheis</span> — the first of the seven nations. <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span> contains all ten levels of holiness, and all <span class="h">mitzvos</span> dependent on bread are there. The essential purification of bread — from <span class="h">chametz</span> to <span class="h">matzah</span>, from <span class="h">cheis</span> to <span class="h">hei</span> — is in <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span>. Therefore the promise of the Land was given when Avraham received the <span class="h">hei</span> in his name: <span class="v">"your name shall no longer be called Avram… and I will give you the land of Canaan"</span> <span class="sr">(Genesis 17:5-8)</span>. The holiness of <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span> = the holiness of <span class="h">da'as</span> — <span class="mx">"the air of <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span> makes one wise"</span> — all in the aspect of <span class="h">hei</span>.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 7 — Conquering <i>Eretz Yisrael</i> and Eating with Joy</div>

<div class="p">Avraham purchased the field from <span class="h">Bnei Cheis</span> specifically — conquering <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span> is through breaking the <span class="h">cheis</span>: <span class="v">"rebuke the beast of the reed [<span class="h">cheis kaneh</span>]"</span> = Esav who destroyed the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span> and exiled Israel. One merits <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span> through breaking <span class="h">cheis kaneh</span> → <span class="h">hei</span> = <span class="h">matzah</span> = holy eating.</div>

<div class="p">One must eat with joy: <span class="v">"go, eat your bread with joy"</span> <span class="sr">(Ecclesiastes 9:7)</span>. When eating in holiness (= <span class="h">matzah</span>), one merits thunder, and the crookedness of the heart is straightened — <span class="v">"he ate and drank, and his heart was good"</span> <span class="sr">(Ruth 3:7)</span> = opposite the crookedness. Then: <span class="v">"for the upright of heart — joy"</span> = opposite <span class="v">"in sorrow you shall eat."</span> Holy eating = rectification of Adam's sin: the sorrow is sweetened into joy.</div>

<div class="p" style="font-style:italic;color:var(--section-num);text-align:center;margin-top:1rem;">[Betzi'as HaPas Halachah 4 is included in Netilas Yadayim Halachah 4.]</div>

<!-- Translator's Summary -->
<div class="ts">
  <div class="sh">✦ Translator's Summary ✦</div>

  <p style="margin-bottom:.8rem;"><strong>Seudah Halachah 1</strong> (LM I:29): Wine exempts all beverages. Wine = <em>da'as</em> at the highest level. Prayer before eating rectifies the blood/<em>nefesh</em>. All blessings rectify the <em>nefashos</em> clothed in food. Wine's <em>da'as</em> is so great that bread cannot exempt it. <em>HaTov V'HaMeitiv</em> on a change of wine — where there is <em>da'as</em>, there is good.</p>

  <p style="margin-bottom:.8rem;"><strong>Seudah Halachah 2</strong> (LM II:2): All blessings on enjoyment include this world in the World to Come. <em>Hoda'ah</em> = World to Come = <em>lashon hakodesh</em> = drawing Shabbos into weekdays = revealing the simple unity from diverse actions. <strong>Specific blessings are greater than general ones</strong> — the more particular the food, the more precious the revelation of unity. Bread and wine establish their own blessings because they contain the most <em>da'as</em>. Five grains = the <em>hei</em> of <em>da'as</em>. Bread accompanies the <em>Korban Todah</em> — the only offering that endures in the future.</p>

  <p style="margin-bottom:.8rem;"><strong>Betzi'as HaPas Halachah 3</strong> (LM I:5): The mystery of why all laws of <em>HaMotzi</em> are named after "breaking." The <em>cheis</em> of <em>chametz</em> (ח) is broken into the <em>hei</em> of <em>matzah</em> (ה). Breaking bread after the blessing = extracting bread from <em>chametz</em> to <em>matzah</em>, from death to life. <em>Lechem oni</em> = the poor man's broken piece = <em>tzedakah</em> = adding <em>hei</em>. A mourner cannot break bread himself. Eating in holiness → thunder → prayer with power → the heart hears. "Begin <em>HaMotzi</em> where the baking began" = where fire/purification started. Avraham purchased from <em>Bnei Cheis</em> = breaking the <em>cheis</em> → <em>hei</em> → conquest of <em>Eretz Yisrael</em>. "Eat your bread with joy" — holy eating straightens the heart's crookedness.</p>
</div>

